Lab Setup
Open a terminal window
Verify your environment:
uname -a
podman --version
Task 1: Output Redirection
Subtask 1.1: Basic Output Redirection (>)
Create a simple text file using output redirection:

echo "Hello, Red Hat OpenShift!" > greeting.txt
Verify the file creation and content:

cat greeting.txt
Expected Output:

Hello, Red Hat OpenShift!
Overwrite the file with new content:

echo "New content replaces old" > greeting.txt
cat greeting.txt
Key Concept: The > operator creates a new file or overwrites existing content.

Subtask 1.2: Appending Output (>>)
Append additional content to your file:

echo "Additional line" >> greeting.txt
cat greeting.txt
Append multiple lines:

echo "Line 3" >> greeting.txt
echo "Line 4" >> greeting.txt
cat greeting.txt
Expected Output:

New content replaces old
Additional line
Line 3
Line 4
Troubleshooting Tip: If you don't see the expected output, verify you used >> instead of >.

Task 2: Using Pipes
Subtask 2.1: Basic Pipe Usage
List files and filter results:

ls -l | grep "greeting"
Count lines in a file:

cat greeting.txt | wc -l
Combine multiple pipes:

ps aux | grep podman | wc -l
Explanation: This shows how many podman processes are running by:

Listing all processes (ps aux)
Filtering for podman (grep podman)
Counting results (wc -l)
Subtask 2.2: Advanced Pipe Operations
Sort file contents:

cat greeting.txt | sort
Create a sorted version of the file:

cat greeting.txt | sort > sorted_greeting.txt
Podman Example: List container images and filter:

podman images | grep -v "<none>"
Task 3: Error Redirection
Subtask 3.1: Redirecting Standard Error (2>)
Generate an error and redirect it:

ls /nonexistent 2> error.log
cat error.log
Combine stdout and stderr redirection:

ls /nonexistent /etc/passwd > output.log 2> error.log
Check both files:

cat output.log
cat error.log
Subtask 3.2: Advanced Error Handling
Redirect both stdout and stderr to the same file:

ls /nonexistent /etc/passwd &> combined.log
Discard error messages completely:

ls /nonexistent 2> /dev/null
Podman Example: Handle container errors:

podman run --name testcontainer alpine /bin/false 2> container_error.log
cat container_error.log
Task 4: Combined Operations
Subtask 4.1: Complex Redirection
Count successful and failed commands:

(ls /etc/passwd /nonexistent | wc -l > success.log) 2> fail.log
View results:

cat success.log
cat fail.log
Subtask 4.2: Practical Application
Create a system report:

{
  echo "=== System Report ==="
  date
  echo "=== Memory ==="
  free -h
  echo "=== Disk Usage ==="
  df -h
} > system_report.txt 2> system_errors.log
Podman Example: Generate container report:

{
  echo "=== Container Images ==="
  podman images
  echo "=== Running Containers ==="
  podman ps
} > container_report.txt 2> container_errors.log
